      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Social Capital Theory has witnessed significant evolution since its inception, with key authors contributing to its development and refinement. While sociologist Pierre Bourdieu and metals sociologist Robert Putnam are often credited with initiating the concept, subsequent research has profoundly impacted our understanding. Authors such as Coleman, Lin, Portes, and Granovetter have expanded the theory by examining the relationship between social capital, social networks, and outcomes like economic growth, health, and civic engagement (Coleman, 1988; Lin, 2001; Portes & Sensenbrenner, 1990). These studies have expanded our knowledge of intergenerational relationships, trust, and civic engagement. Burt's network analysis, which focuses on structural holes, has also expanded theoretical frameworks encompassing multiple indices (Burt, 1992). Schnepf and Austin suggest augmenting existing theories through Southwest migration experiences, which emphasizes efficacy levels past network membership (Field, 1995; Woolcock, 1995). In recent years, nuanced discussions around trust and unequal representations have emerged, further reconstructing contemporary debates (Franklin, 2005).",
      "source": "target",
